          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Hesiod's Life and Times</strong><br>\nHesiod was an ancient Greek poet who lived around 750 to 650 BCE. His works are a major source for our understanding of Greek mythology, farming techniques, and ancient time-keeping. Hesiod is often considered a contemporary of Homer and like Homer, little is known about his life. His poetry suggests he lived in Boeotia, a region in Central Greece, where he worked as a shepherd and farmer.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Works and Days</strong><br>\nWorks and Days is one of Hesiod's most famous works. It is a poem that provides insights into the daily life and work of ancient Greeks. Through this work, Hesiod gives advice on farming, seafaring, and even morality. The poem blends practical work advice with mythological elements, showing a world where humans are closely connected to the gods and the rhythms of nature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Theogony and Mythology</strong><br>\nAnother significant work of Hesiod is the \"Theogony,\" which details the origins and genealogy of the Greek gods. This work is our key source for Greek mythology, containing the first recorded cosmogony—explanation of the universe's origins. \"Theogony\" offers stories about the birth of the gods, their battles for supremacy, and the establishment of their roles in the cosmos.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
