          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Hesiod's Writing Style</strong><br>\nHesiod's style is didactic and direct, using the hexameter verse form that is also found in epic poetry. His language is often moralistic, providing lessons and warnings to his audience. Hesiod's poetry is characterized by its attention to detail and a keen observation of the agricultural calendar and the natural world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Greek Culture</strong><br>\nHesiod's writings had a profound impact on Greek culture and were considered as important as Homer's epics in classical education. His works inform us not only about mythology but also about ancient Greek values and their view of justice, work ethics, and the divine.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hesiod and Justice</strong><br>\nJustice is a central theme in Hesiod's \"Works and Days.\" He speaks of \"Dike,\" the goddess of justice, and emphasizes the importance of fair dealings and honesty in life. Hesiod portrays justice as a crucial element for social order and prosperity in the community.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
